<!DOCTYPE topic
        SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="Server" id="grpc-server">

    <p>
        <code>GrpcServer</code> listens for incoming gRPC calls and dispatches them to registered service implementations.
    </p>
    <code-block lang="Kotlin">
        val server = GrpcServer(8080) {
            services {
                registerService&lt;ImageRecognizer&gt; { ImageRecognizerImpl() }
            }
        }

        server.start()
        server.awaitTermination()
    </code-block>
    <p>
        Multiple services can be registered in the same <code>services</code> block:
    </p>
    <code-block lang="Kotlin">
        val server = GrpcServer(8080) {
            services {
                registerService&lt;ImageRecognizer&gt; { ImageRecognizerImpl() }
                registerService&lt;EchoService&gt; { EchoServiceImpl() }
            }
        }
    </code-block>

    <chapter title="Configuration" id="grpc-server-configuration">
        <deflist>
            <def title="credentials">
                Sets server credentials.
                By default the server uses plaintext.
                Use <code>tls(certificateChain, privateKey) { ... }</code> for secure communication.
            </def>
            <def title="messageMarshallerResolver">
                A <code>GrpcMarshallerResolver</code> for custom serialization.
                Not needed when using generated Protobuf types.
                See <a href="grpc-services.topic" anchor="no-proto"/>.
            </def>
            <def title="intercept(...)">
                Adds server-side interceptors for cross-cutting concerns like authentication,
                logging, or metrics.
            </def>
            <def title="fallbackHandlerRegistry">
                A custom <code>GrpcHandlerRegistry</code> for resolving services that were not
                registered via <code>services { ... }</code>.
                Unresolved services return <code>UNIMPLEMENTED</code> status by default.
            </def>
            <def title="maxInboundMessageSize">
                The maximum size, in bytes, of an inbound message.
                A larger message fails the call with <code>RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED</code>.
                Defaults to 4 MiB (4,194,304 bytes).
            </def>
            <def title="maxInboundMetadataSize">
                The maximum size, in bytes, of inbound metadata.
                Defaults to 8 KiB (8,192 bytes).
            </def>
            <def title="keepAlive { ... }">
                Configures server-side keep-alive pings to detect broken connections.
                The server sends pings on all connections.

                <deflist>
                    <def title="time">
                        The time without read activity after which the server sends a keepalive ping.
                        The default is two hours. With <code>Duration.INFINITE</code>, the server disables the pings.
                        Finite values must be at least 10 seconds.
                    </def>
                    <def title="timeout">
                        The time that the server waits for read activity after a keepalive ping.
                        Defaults to 20 seconds. With <code>Duration.INFINITE</code>, the server waits without limit.
                        Finite values must be at least 10 milliseconds.
                    </def>
                </deflist>
            </def>
            <def title="maxConnectionIdle">
                *Sets the maximum connection idle time.
                When a connection exceeds this idle time, the server begins to close it gracefully.*
                Defaults to <code>Duration.INFINITE</code> (no limit).
            </def>
            <def title="maxConnectionAge">
                Sets the maximum connection age.
                When a connection reaches this age, the server begins to close it gracefully.
                Defaults to <code>Duration.INFINITE</code> (no limit).
            </def>
            <def title="maxConnectionAgeGrace">
                Sets the grace time for the graceful connection termination.
                Once the <code>maxConnectionAge</code> is reached, RPCs have the grace time to complete.
                RPCs that do not complete in time will be cancelled, allowing the connection to terminate.
                Defaults to <code>Duration.INFINITE</code>, which lets active RPCs run to completion.
            </def>
        </deflist>
        <p>
            Independent of <code>keepAlive</code>, the server limits how often a client can send keep-alive pings:
        </p>
        <list>
            <li>On a connection with active calls: one ping every 5 minutes.</li>
            <li>
                On a connection without active calls: one ping every 2 hours on the JVM,
                and one every 5 minutes on Kotlin/Native.
            </li>
        </list>
        <p>
            These options are currently not configurable.
        </p>
        <p>
            The limits restart each time the server sends response headers or data.
            A client that repeatedly pings more often loses its connection.
            The server sends <code>GOAWAY</code> with the debug data <code>too_many_pings</code>.
        </p>
        <note>
            On the JVM, the server provider must support <code>keepAlive</code>, <code>maxConnectionIdle</code>,
            <code>maxConnectionAge</code>, and <code>maxConnectionAgeGrace</code>.
            If the provider doesn't support these options, server creation throws a runtime exception.
            The Netty provider supports these options.
        </note>
        <note>
            Finite durations must be less than <code>Int.MAX_VALUE</code> milliseconds (about 24.8 days).
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Lifecycle" id="grpc-server-lifecycle">
        <p>
            Call <code>start()</code> to begin listening for connections.
            Use <code>shutdown()</code> for an orderly shutdown (existing calls finish,
            new calls are rejected) or <code>shutdownNow()</code> to cancel everything immediately.
            <code>awaitTermination()</code> blocks until all calls have completed.
        </p>
    </chapter>
</topic>
